1
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:07,910
Now that we have seen a valid solution to
the critical section problem, let's look into

2
00:00:07,910 --> 00:00:11,340
how hardware can help us in this context.

3
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:17,330
As mentioned in previous lectures, collaboration
between the OS and the hardware is essential

4
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:23,440
and it can be observed in many modules and
aspects of modern OSs.

5
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:30,170
The first option we look at is a function
that allows users to disable/enable interrupts

6
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:34,289
on one specific core, as shown in the slide.

7
00:00:34,289 --> 00:00:39,079
Such a function could in principle help, but
you probably agree with me that disabling

8
00:00:39,079 --> 00:00:44,530
interrupts can lead to limited ability of
interleaving programs.

9
00:00:44,530 --> 00:00:49,930
In a sense, the user might be given "too much
power" and affect/limit the OS control.

10
00:00:49,930 --> 00:00:55,030
More importantly, the problem with this solution
is that by disabling the interrupts on one

11
00:00:55,030 --> 00:01:00,170
core, threads running on different cores are
still able to lead to behavior that does not

12
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:03,940
satisfy the critical section problem.

13
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:09,460
Something better is provided by Read-Modify-Write
(RMW) operations.

14
00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:15,940
In this case we are not talking of individual
atomic reads or writes, but of an atomic operation

15
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:18,600
that performs both a read and a write.

16
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:24,250
Examples of these operations are TestAndSet,
FetchAndAdd, CompareAndSwap, and so on.

17
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:28,270
We will look at some of these next.

18
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:31,680
Let's try with the TestAndSet (TAS) instruction.

19
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:38,450
As shown in the slide, the function gets a
boolean variable, sets it to true and returns

20
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:39,640
the previous value.

21
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:46,749
As I said, this instruction is executed atomically,
so it either runs in its entirety or it does

22
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:47,979
not.

23
00:01:47,979 --> 00:01:53,429
Under the hood, the hardware is either disabling
interrupts if the system is uniprocessor or

24
00:01:53,429 --> 00:01:56,880
locking the system bus if the system is a
multiprocessor one.

25
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:02,920
Now, if you think about it, you will agree
with me that if the target variable is set

26
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:11,950
to false and N concurrent threads invoke the
TAS, then only one will get false in return.

27
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:17,510
If you agree with this, then probably you
can see how we can use the TAS in the entry

28
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:20,480
section as shown in the example.

29
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:27,670
A thread can enter its critical section only
if the TAS returns false.

30
00:02:27,670 --> 00:02:33,340
If there are concurrent invocations, the TAS
will only return false to one thread, and

31
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:38,460
no other thread will be able to enter its
critical section until another thread exits

32
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:41,120
its own and sets lock to false.

33
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:47,860
Please, take a moment and try to reason formally
about whether this solution meets the mutual

34
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,459
exclusion, progress and fairness properties.

35
00:02:50,459 --> 00:02:55,349
An alternative instruction we could use is
the CompareAndSwap (CAS).

36
00:02:55,349 --> 00:02:59,980
In this case, the instructions that are run
atomically will set a given value to a given

37
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:05,140
variable only if the current value of such
variable is the expected one.

38
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:09,460
If you look at this alternative formulation
of the critical section, you will see it's

39
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:13,560
equivalent to the one using the TAS.

40
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:19,420
Something worth noticing about these solutions
is that they are busy waiting / spinning.

41
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:26,120
Each thread will keep consuming CPU cycles
until it manages to enter its critical section,

42
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:29,620
which is a waste of resources.

43
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:33,909
If you have tried to argue yourself about
whether these are valid solutions to the critical

44
00:03:33,909 --> 00:03:39,580
section problem, you probably have reached
the conclusion that from the fairness perspective,

45
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:41,799
they are also not optimal.

46
00:03:41,799 --> 00:03:46,549
More concretely, they can lead to starvation,
with the same thread being the one that keeps

47
00:03:46,549 --> 00:03:52,430
entering its critical section and other threads
not managing to do so.

48
00:03:52,430 --> 00:03:57,930
The third point to think about is the underlying
OS scheduling mechanism.

49
00:03:57,930 --> 00:04:02,689
Even if in this lecture we have not really
made any assumption on the scheduling mechanism

50
00:04:02,689 --> 00:04:08,670
the underlying OS is using, you should not
think we do not need to make assumptions when

51
00:04:08,670 --> 00:04:10,909
looking at a specific solution.

52
00:04:10,909 --> 00:04:17,280
For instance, if we assume the OS is running
a non-preemptive priority-based scheduler

53
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:22,630
and the thread that is spinning in its entry
section is the one with the highest priority,

54
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:29,260
then the solution can lead to a deadlock and
no thread is actually able to make any progess.

55
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:36,600
If we now go back to the observation about
starvation, let's look at an alternative formulation

56
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:39,470
that can help us achieve better fairness.

57
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:43,420
Let's assume the entry and exit sections are
as shown in this slide.

58
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:52,310
Besides a shared lock variable initially set
to false, we have a shared array of boolean

59
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:54,360
values called waiting.

60
00:04:54,360 --> 00:04:58,330
The array has one boolean per thread, initially
set to false.

61
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:03,600
In its entry section, before entering the
while loop, each thread shares its will of

62
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:07,223
entering the critical section by setting its
entry in the waiting array to true.

63
00:05:07,223 --> 00:05:15,830
The thread will stay in the loop until it
manages to run TAS (as before) and while its

64
00:05:15,830 --> 00:05:18,360
waiting entry remains true.

65
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:25,480
After exiting the while loop and before entering
its critical section, the thread sets its

66
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:28,140
entry in waiting to false.

67
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:33,710
Now let's look at what happens after the thread
exits its critical section.

68
00:05:33,710 --> 00:05:39,050
The thread circles over the waiting array,
starting from the next thread in the array

69
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:46,340
(as if the buffer is circular) and then stops
as soon as it finds one entry for which waiting

70
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:50,570
is true or if completes the circle.

71
00:05:50,570 --> 00:05:56,190
If no other thread is waiting in its loop
inside its entry section, then this thread

72
00:05:56,190 --> 00:06:02,060
sets lock to false, so some other thread that
wants to enter its critical section later

73
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:04,010
on can do so.

74
00:06:04,010 --> 00:06:09,400
Notice that the thread that enters its critical
section next could be the thread that just

75
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:14,280
set lock to false, but this cannot happen
for an unbounded number of times (if another

76
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:18,960
thread is waiting), since if another thread
also wants to enter the critical section,

77
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:24,480
then its entry in waiting will be true and
the thread that manages to enter its critical

78
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:31,300
section will then hand over the lock in its
exit section, as shown in line 14.

79
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:38,460
As before, try to formally reason about whether
this solution is valid or not by yourselves.

